+\begin{remark*}[Subsequent expositions]
+Several expositions and variants of the argument of this paper have
+since appeared. Banks \cite{Banks} gives a detailed direct proof with
+explicit constants: for conductors $q\ge10^{10}$ one may take
+$c=3.0\times10^{-3}$, and combined with the numerical verification of
+Lu, Zaman, and Zhao \cite{LZZ} this covers every conductor $q\ge3$.
+Kerr \cite{Kerr} works in the same biquadratic field
+$\Q(\sqrt{d},\sqrt{2})$ as here, but replaces the interpolation lemma and the
+determinant analysis by a pigeonhole construction of a polynomial of
+bounded degree vanishing at the relevant linear forms, which is then
+contradicted by a zero estimate of Masser \cite{Masser}. Green
+\cite{Green} gives a variant that works entirely in the quadratic field
+$\Q(\sqrt{d})$ attached to $\chi$, avoiding the passage to a biquadratic
+field. There the interpolation determinant is replaced by the explicit
+Gram determinant of a family of evaluation vectors, whose archimedean
+size and $p$-adic valuations at primes with $\chi(p)=-1$ are compared
+directly.
+\end{remark*}
